#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "CAP-REPOSITORIES Repositories by trust"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Each layer is its own repository and the unit of trust for its CI identities."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Layer L0 · domain: delivery"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Purpose"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "The code that defines who may change the platform is kept apart from the code that changes it, so you can trust each repository for exactly what it holds."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Why"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "With workload identity federation, a credential trusts a repository, so the repository is the unit of trust ([P2]{1}). In a single monorepo, anyone who could edit a pipeline could reach every identity. Pinned recipes give the same result on any machine and on any CI host ([P19]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "A bootstrap repository, holding the state store and the CI identities, applied on dispatch."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "A platform repository, holding the shared stacks and the tenant registry, applied on merge."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "A modules repository, whose tagged releases the others use."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Product repositories, one for each product, holding its code, jobs and deployment unit."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-repositories.md
msgid "It's normal to have two remotes. A repository can be mirrored on a second host and carry that host's CI definition alongside the first one. Only the wrapper differs, because every step is a recipe."
msgstr ""

